/// \defgroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil WWLib random numbers and C-string helpers
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief Westwood's four small random-number generators, the template
///        that draws a number in a range from any of them, and five
///        C-string and integer helpers: strtrim, wcstrim, strtok_r, nstrdup
///        and the GCD and LCM functions.
///
/// All of this is general-purpose WWLib code with no game rules in it. Every
/// function documented here is a matched ledger row whose body is Zero
/// Hour's WWLib source. The one change is in nstrdup: its allocation passes
/// the tag 'str' through the tagged array operator new. Plain new[] reaches
/// the same game allocator with no tag. BFME 2's tree has no copy of the
/// declaring headers: its units compile against the Open-BFME-1 reference
/// copies of random.h, trim.h, strtok_r.h and gcd_lcm.h.
///
/// Who uses it in BFME 2, from the matched callers unless noted:
/// - Random3Class and Random4Class feed W3D presentation code: particle
///   buffers, randomised texture mappers, the WWMath Vector3Randomizer
///   shapes, and the jitter on segment lines and streaks. RandomClass and
///   Random2Class have no named direct caller so far.
/// - Particle emitters reach Random3Class only indirectly, through their
///   Vector3Randomizer creation volumes (virtual Get_Vector calls, read from
///   source rather than from matched callers).
/// - strtrim and nstrdup serve WWLib's INIClass (Westwood's own INI reader),
///   and both trim functions serve Read_Line. strtok_r splits GameSpy chat
///   commands and the lobby game-options string. Greatest_Common_Divisor is
///   used by the W3D particle emitter.
///
/// These generators are not the logic random-number generator. The values
/// that must agree on every machine in a lockstep game come from
/// GetGameLogicRandomValue and GetGameLogicRandomValueReal. Those, like the
/// client and audio variants, draw from randomValue, a separate multi-word
/// add-with-carry generator, and never from these classes.
/// \ref sub_wwlib_thirdparty describes the rest of WWLib.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here is data-driven. The generators have fixed algorithms. Their
///   shared instances (in Vector3Randomizer, ParticleBufferClass and
///   mapper.cpp) are default-constructed and never reassigned in the
///   reconstructed source, so their seeds are fixed.
/// - Apart from Random2Class's own constructor, every matched caller of the
///   generators is W3D or WWMath rendering code. As far as traced, their
///   values change only what is drawn and are not part of the lockstep or
///   CRC state. That covers only the direct, named callers found so far:
///   calls through virtual functions and from unnamed code have not all
///   been traced.
/// - Westwood INI text, such as the texture-mapper arguments stored in a W3D
///   vertex material, is trimmed by strtrim. Characters 1 to 32 (control
///   characters and space) count as blanks, and bytes from 128 up do not.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The generators are portable C++ with no platform calls. Random3Class
///   does its arithmetic in signed int. It relies on overflow wrapping at
///   32 bits and on right shifts of negative values sign-extending, which
///   MSVC provides. A port that wants the same sequences should do that
///   arithmetic in unsigned 32-bit and emulate the sign-extending shifts.
/// - strtok_r is compiled only when _UNIX is not defined, because POSIX C
///   libraries already provide a function with this name.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/random.h
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief WWLib's random-number header: the four generator classes and the
///        Pick_Random_Number template.
///
/// BFME 2 has no copy of this header. Its units compile against the
/// Open-BFME-1 reference copy, which is listed here only so that the
/// declarations can be documented. The reference copy matches Zero Hour's
/// RANDOM.H apart from comments and Random3Class's constructor. That
/// constructor is defined inline in the class, and a unit can ask for an
/// out-of-line call instead, because BFME 1 retail inlines it in some units
/// and calls it in others. In BFME 2, seglinerenderer.cpp requests the
/// out-of-line call.
/// Each class's SIGNIFICANT_BITS value tells Pick_Random_Number how many
/// low bits of a draw are random: 15 for RandomClass and 32 for the others.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/random.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief Bodies of the WWLib random-number classes, plus the four explicit
///        instantiations of Pick_Random_Number that retail contains.
///
/// The class declarations and the Pick_Random_Number template are in WWLib's
/// random.h, which BFME 2 takes from the Open-BFME-1 reference tree. Each
/// member operator()(int, int) is a one-line wrapper around its own class's
/// Pick_Random_Number instantiation. That call is also how those four
/// look-alike wrappers were told apart in retail.
/// RandomClass's constructor is not defined here: no out-of-line copy of it
/// has been found in retail so far.

/// \class RandomClass
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief The smallest WWLib generator: a 32-bit linear congruential
///        generator that returns 15-bit values (0 to 32767).
///
/// Reading the object as an int (its operator int) or calling it
/// draws the next value. Constructing or assigning it from a number sets
/// the seed. Its whole state is one 32-bit seed, so it is cheap to copy and
/// to restore. Each step multiplies the seed by 1103515245 and adds 12345,
/// the constants of the classic C library rand(), and returns bits 10 to 24
/// of the result.
///
/// No named BFME 2 function calls its members directly so far.
/// Pick_Random_Number for this class has the step compiled in.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's notes in random.cpp record that this generator failed
///       almost all of the DIEHARD statistical tests. Use it only where
///       quality does not matter.
/// \see Random2Class, Random3Class, Random4Class, Pick_Random_Number

/// \fn int RandomClass::operator()(void)
/// \brief Advances the seed one step and returns the next 15-bit value.
///
/// The seed becomes seed * 1103515245 + 12345, wrapping at 32 bits. The
/// return value is that new seed shifted right by 10 bits and masked to the
/// class's 15 significant bits, so it is never negative.
/// \return A value from 0 to 32767.
/// \see RandomClass::operator()(int, int)

/// \fn int RandomClass::operator()(int minval, int maxval)
/// \brief Returns a value from minval to maxval inclusive. It forwards to
///        Pick_Random_Number for this class.
/// \param minval One end of the range (inclusive).
/// \param maxval The other end of the range (inclusive). The two ends may be
///               given in either order.
/// \return A value in the range.
/// \warning With only 15 significant bits, ranges wider than 32767 are not
///          covered. See Pick_Random_Number.

/// \class Random2Class
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief A fast 32-bit generator that XORs table entries: the R250
///        shift-register construction over a 250-entry table.
///
/// The object holds the table and two read positions 103 entries apart, so
/// one instance is about 1 KB. Each draw is one XOR and two index
/// increments, with no multiplication. The constructor fills the table from
/// a Random3Class seeded with the caller's seed. Reading the object as an int
/// draws the next value.
///
/// No named BFME 2 function calls its members directly so far. Zero Hour's
/// WWLib uses it inside its CRandom and RandomStringClass helpers.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's header warns that the sequence breaks down in about
///       64 dimensions. Its random.cpp notes say it passed the DIEHARD tests
///       but repeats with a short period, with a large spike at 300,000
///       samples in 64 dimensions in the Monte Carlo tests.
/// \see Random3Class, Pick_Random_Number

/// \fn Random2Class::Random2Class(unsigned seed)
/// \brief Seeds the 250-entry table from a Random3Class built with the
///        given seed, and sets the two read positions to 0 and 103.
///
/// The table is filled with the first 250 outputs of
/// Random3Class(seed, 0). Equal seeds therefore always give the same
/// sequence. The default seed is 0.
/// \param seed Seed for the Random3Class that fills the table.
/// \see Random2Class::operator()(void)

/// \fn int Random2Class::operator()(void)
/// \brief Returns the next 32-bit value: the entry at the first position,
///        after it has been XORed with the entry 103 places ahead.
///
/// The first entry keeps the XOR result, so the table evolves as it is read.
/// Both positions then advance and wrap at 250. Any 32-bit pattern can come
/// out, including negative values.
/// \return The new table entry, any int value.

/// \fn int Random2Class::operator()(int minval, int maxval)
/// \brief Returns a value from minval to maxval inclusive. It forwards to
///        Pick_Random_Number for this class.
/// \param minval One end of the range (inclusive).
/// \param maxval The other end of the range (inclusive).
/// \return A value in the range.

/// \class Random3Class
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief A counter-based 32-bit generator: each value is a four-round
///        hash of a fixed seed and a running index.
///
/// The state is two ints: the seed, which never changes, and an index that
/// goes up by one per draw. The n-th value therefore depends only on
/// (seed, n), so a stream can be restarted or repositioned exactly by
/// building a new object with the same two numbers. The constructor is
/// inline in random.h, and both arguments default to 0.
///
/// BFME 2 users, from matched callers:
/// - Vector3Randomizer keeps one shared static Random3Class. The WWMath
///   randomizer shapes (solid box, solid sphere, hollow sphere and solid
///   cylinder) draw from it.
/// - SegLineRendererClass::subdivision_util and
///   StreakRendererClass::subdivision_util create a local, default-seeded
///   Random3Class and use it when the line's random offsets are frozen.
///   The frozen jitter therefore repeats exactly from call to call.
///   Otherwise they draw from the shared randomizer.
/// - Random2Class's constructor uses one to fill its table.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's notes in random.cpp record 11 failed DIEHARD tests out
///       of 253 and strong bias from two dimensions up, although the header
///       describes the output as approaching cryptographic quality.
/// \see Random2Class, Pick_Random_Number

/// \fn int Random3Class::operator()(void)
/// \brief Hashes (seed, index) into a 32-bit value and advances the index.
///
/// The low word starts as the seed and the high word as the index. Four
/// rounds then each mix the high word with a constant from the static
/// Mix1 table: the 16-bit halves are squared, the result is half-swapped
/// and combined with a constant from Mix2, and the words trade places. The
/// final high word is returned.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The round structure and the first four entries of Mix1 and Mix2 match
/// the "pseudo-DES" hash published in Numerical Recipes (psdes). The
/// tables hold 20 entries each, but only the first four are read. The
/// arithmetic is done in signed int, so the right shifts sign-extend
/// negative values. The output therefore differs from the textbook
/// unsigned version. Keep that in mind when porting or checking it
/// against a reference.
/// \return Any int value.

/// \fn int Random3Class::operator()(int minval, int maxval)
/// \brief Returns a value from minval to maxval inclusive. It forwards to
///        Pick_Random_Number for this class.
/// \param minval One end of the range (inclusive).
/// \param maxval The other end of the range (inclusive).
/// \return A value in the range.

/// \class Random4Class
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief A Mersenne Twister (MT19937) generator: 624 words of state, 32-bit
///        output, and a floating-point helper.
///
/// This is WWLib's best-quality generator. Its BFME 2 users, from matched
/// callers:
/// - ParticleBufferClass keeps a file-static instance. Its Reset_Colors,
///   Reset_Opacity, Reset_Size, Reset_Rotations, Reset_Frames and
///   Reset_Blur_Times functions fill the per-buffer tables of random
///   variation from it.
/// - mapper.cpp's global instance drives RandomTextureMapperClass through
///   Random4Class::Get_Float. It sets a random rotation and centre each time
///   the mapper re-randomises.
///
/// Both instances are default-constructed at static-initialisation time, so
/// they start from the same seed in every run.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's notes in random.cpp record that it passed the DIEHARD
///       tests and the Monte Carlo tests up to 96 dimensions. The header warns
///       that it is not for cryptography.
/// \see Random4Class::Get_Float, Pick_Random_Number

/// \fn Random4Class::Random4Class(unsigned int seed)
/// \brief Fills the 624-word state from one seed, using the original 1998
///        Mersenne Twister seeding.
///
/// The first word is the seed. Each following word is the previous word
/// times 69069, wrapping at 32 bits. The index is left at 624, so the first
/// draw regenerates the whole block.
/// \param seed Starting seed. The default argument in random.h is 4357. A
///             seed of 0 is replaced by 4375, not 4357, so
///             Random4Class(0) and Random4Class() give different sequences.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Swapping in std::mt19937 does not reproduce these sequences. Generation
/// and tempering are the standard MT19937 steps, but std::mt19937 seeds with
/// the later recurrence (multiplier 1812433253), not this one.
/// \see Random4Class::operator()(void)

/// \fn int Random4Class::operator()(void)
/// \brief Returns the next tempered 32-bit Mersenne Twister output.
///
/// When all 624 words have been used, the whole block is regenerated at
/// once with the standard MT19937 twist. The next word
/// is then tempered with the standard shifts and masks, and its bits are
/// returned unchanged as an int.
/// \return Any int value. The bit pattern is uniformly distributed over 32
///         bits.
/// \see Random4Class::Get_Float

/// \fn int Random4Class::operator()(int minval, int maxval)
/// \brief Returns a value from minval to maxval inclusive. It forwards to
///        Pick_Random_Number for this class.
/// \param minval One end of the range (inclusive).
/// \param maxval The other end of the range (inclusive).
/// \return A value in the range.

/// \fn float Random4Class::Get_Float()
/// \brief Returns the next output scaled to the range 0 to 1.
///
/// The next 32-bit output is read as unsigned and multiplied by the float
/// constant 1/(2^32 - 1), which as a float is exactly 2^-32.
/// RandomTextureMapperClass is the matched caller: it uses the
/// result for its random rotation angle and texture centre.
/// \return A float from 0 to 1. A float keeps only 24 significant bits, so
///         the largest outputs round to exactly 1.0. Do not treat the upper
///         bound as exclusive.

/// \fn template<class T> int Pick_Random_Number(T &generator, int minval, int maxval)
/// \brief Draws a value from minval to maxval inclusive from any WWLib
///        generator, by masking and rejection.
///
/// One template, defined in random.h. BFME 2's random.cpp explicitly
/// instantiates it for RandomClass, Random2Class, Random3Class and
/// Random4Class. Retail contains all four copies, and the only named caller
/// of each is its own class's member operator()(int, int). In the RandomClass
/// and Random2Class copies the generator step is compiled in. The other
/// two call their generator.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - If minval equals maxval, that value is returned and the generator is
///   not advanced. If the ends are reversed, they are swapped.
/// - The magnitude is maxval - minval. The highest set bit of the magnitude
///   is searched for, starting from the generator's top significant bit:
///   bit 14 for RandomClass and bit 31 for the others. A mask with every bit
///   up to the bit found is built.
/// - The generator is read and masked until the result is no greater than
///   the magnitude. The result is then added to minval. Each draw is
///   accepted with probability above one half, so the loop runs fewer than
///   two times on average. How many values it consumes varies, so the
///   generator's position afterwards depends on the ranges requested.
///
/// \param generator The generator to read. It is advanced by one or more
///                  draws, or by none when the range is a single value.
/// \param minval    One end of the range (inclusive).
/// \param maxval    The other end of the range (inclusive).
/// \return A value from min(minval, maxval) to max(minval, maxval).
///
/// \warning For RandomClass (15 significant bits), the bit search sees only
///          the low 15 bits of the magnitude, so a range wider than 32767
///          is not covered. With minval 0, a maxval of 40000 gives only
///          0 to 8191, and a maxval of 65536 gives only 0 or 1.
/// \warning maxval - minval must fit in an int. Wider ranges overflow and
///          are not handled.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/trim.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief strtrim and wcstrim: trim blanks from both ends of a C string in
///        place.
///
/// Zero Hour's trim.cpp, unchanged. The declarations are in WWLib's trim.h.

/// \fn char *strtrim(char *buffer)
/// \brief Removes leading and trailing blanks from a narrow C string, in
///        place.
///
/// Westwood's INI and line-reading code uses it to clean keys, values and
/// lines. Matched callers include INIClass::Load(Straw&),
/// INIClass::Get_String, INIClass::Strip_Comments, INIClass::Get_Point,
/// INIClass::Get_Rect, INIClass::Get_UUBlock, INIClass::Get_Wide_String,
/// INIClass::Put_TextBlock and the narrow Read_Line(Straw&, char*, int, bool&).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Any character whose unsigned value is 1 to 32 counts as a blank. That
/// covers space, tab, carriage return, line feed and the other control
/// characters. Leading blanks are skipped, and the rest of the string is
/// copied down to the start of the buffer with strcpy. Trailing blanks are
/// then overwritten with terminators, working back from the end.
///
/// \par Retail quirk
/// After the shift, the trailing pass keeps going only while the character
/// at index k is not a terminator, where k is the number of leading blanks
/// removed. If the trimmed text is shorter than k and the shifted string is
/// at least k characters long, the pass clears index k and stops, leaving
/// k minus the text length trailing blanks. If the shifted string is exactly
/// k long, nothing is cleared. If it is shorter than k, index k still holds
/// the old first non-blank character and trimming completes. Examples: two
/// spaces, "a" and one space give "a" and one space; four spaces, "ab" and
/// three spaces give "ab" and two spaces; five spaces, "a" and two spaces
/// give "a" alone. Zero Hour has the same code.
///
/// \param buffer The string to trim. It is modified in place. Null is
///               allowed and is returned unchanged.
/// \return buffer.
/// \see wcstrim

/// \fn wchar_t *wcstrim(wchar_t *buffer)
/// \brief Removes leading and trailing blanks from a wide C string, in place.
///
/// The wide-character twin of strtrim, with the same rules, the same use of
/// wcscpy for the shift, and the same early-stop quirk. A blank is any
/// character with a code from 1 to 32, so Unicode spaces such as U+00A0
/// and U+3000 are kept. Its matched caller is the wide
/// Read_Line(Straw&, wchar_t*, int, bool&).
/// \param buffer The string to trim, modified in place. Null is allowed.
/// \return buffer.
/// \see strtrim

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/strtok_r.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief strtok_r: a re-entrant string tokenizer for Windows builds.
///
/// Zero Hour's strtok_r.cpp, unchanged. The declaration is in WWLib's
/// strtok_r.h. The body is compiled only when _UNIX is not defined.

/// \fn char *strtok_r(char *strptr, const char *delimiters, char **lasts)
/// \brief Returns the next token of a string. Unlike strtok, the scan
///        position is kept in the caller's variable, not in a static.
///
/// Because the position lives in *lasts, two tokenizations can be
/// interleaved, and a function that tokenizes can be called in the middle
/// of another tokenization. The delimiter set may differ from call to call.
/// Matched BFME 2 callers:
/// - ParseAsciiStringToGameInfo reads the lobby game-options string. It
///   splits the string into key=value pairs at ';', each pair at '=', the
///   slot list at ':', and each slot's fields at ','.
/// - The GameSpy peer thread's roomMessageCallback and playerMessageCallback
///   split chat command messages at spaces.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Leading delimiters are skipped, so runs of delimiters count as one and an
/// empty token is never returned: "a,,b" split at ',' gives two tokens, not
/// three. The delimiter that ends a token is overwritten with a terminator,
/// and *lasts is moved past it, or to the string's terminator after the last
/// token.
///
/// \param strptr     The string to start tokenizing. It is modified in
///                   place. Pass null to continue from *lasts.
/// \param delimiters The characters that separate tokens.
/// \param lasts      [in,out] The scan position, stored between calls. It
///                   must not be null. On a continuing call, *lasts must
///                   point into a string that an earlier call started.
/// \return The next token, or null when only delimiters or nothing are
///         left.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/nstrdup.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief nstrdup: copies a C string into a block from the engine's own
///        allocator.
///
/// Zero Hour's nstrdup.cpp, with one BFME 2 change: the copy is allocated
/// through the tagged array operator new, which forwards 'str' as the
/// allocation tag. Both it and the plain array new go to the game allocator
/// (memory class 2). Zero Hour: the plain new[] behind W3DNEWARRAY was
/// itself routed to the game's dynamic memory allocator. As in Zero Hour,
/// nstrdup has its own source file.

/// \fn char *nstrdup(const char *str)
/// \brief Returns a newly allocated copy of a C string, or null for a null
///        input.
///
/// This is WWLib's strdup equivalent. The only difference is that the
/// memory comes from operator new[], so it is released with delete[], not
/// free. Callers:
/// - INIClass: both Load overloads and both Save overloads keep a copy of
///   the file name. Initialize, and Clear when it clears the whole file,
///   store the placeholder file name &lt;unknown&gt;.
/// - The W3D NamedPivotMapClass weight table, whose WeightInfoStruct copies
///   its pivot name on assignment and frees it with delete[].
///
/// \par Implementation
/// strlen + 1 bytes are allocated with the tagged array operator new, which
/// passes the request to the game allocator with memory class 2 and the
/// multi-character constant 'str' as the allocation tag. The string is then
/// copied with strcpy. The allocation result is not checked for null.
///
/// \param str The string to copy. Null is allowed.
/// \return The copy, owned by the caller (release with delete[]), or null
///         when str is null.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/gcd_lcm.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil
/// \brief Greatest common divisor and least common multiple of two unsigned
///        ints.
///
/// Zero Hour's gcd_lcm.cpp, unchanged. The declarations are in WWLib's
/// gcd_lcm.h.

/// \fn unsigned int Greatest_Common_Divisor(unsigned int a, unsigned int b)
/// \brief Returns the greatest common divisor of a and b, using Euclid's
///        algorithm.
///
/// The function recurses on (b, a mod b) until b is 0. A
/// GCD of anything with 0 is the other number, and the GCD of 0 and 0 is 0.
/// Least_Common_Multiple uses it. So does
/// ParticleEmitterClass::Create_New_Particles: after a very long frame, it
/// divides the particle buffer's size by the GCD of that size and the
/// burst size. That gives the number of bursts in one full cycle, so whole
/// cycles can be skipped instead of emitted.
/// \param a First value.
/// \param b Second value.
/// \return The greatest common divisor.
/// \see Least_Common_Multiple

/// \fn unsigned int Least_Common_Multiple(unsigned int a, unsigned int b)
/// \brief Returns the least common multiple of a and b, computed as
///        a * b / GCD(a, b).
///
/// No named BFME 2 function calls it directly so far.
/// \param a First value.
/// \param b Second value.
/// \return The least common multiple.
/// \warning The product a * b is formed first and wraps at 32 bits, so the
///          result is wrong whenever that product exceeds 4,294,967,295,
///          even when the true LCM fits. If a and b are both 0, it
///          divides by zero.
/// \see Greatest_Common_Divisor
